WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.876 --> 00:00:05.380
Fusion power has a certain reputation,

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.174
something in the realm of a holy grail,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.051
but not in a good way.

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.554
There is a famous joke
about nuclear fusion

00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:14.639
about how it never becomes a reality.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.017
You can Google it if you like.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:20.353
But really the moment we are
in with nuclear fusion today

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:23.231
is probably more exciting
than it's ever been.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:25.275
There's more activity in the fusion world

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.695
than ever, and not just in
government research labs.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.448
There's also an emerging
private fusion industry

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.700
that's attracted billions
of dollars in capital

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.201
in recent years.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.120
Governments and private investors alike

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.456
realize that we've got to find a solution

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.834
that's going to allow us
to get to net zero targets.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:43.793
This is one of the hardest

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:47.505
but most rewarding problems
that humanity could work on.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:48.965
Ultimately, we all want the same thing.

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:51.176
We want someone to put
electricity on the grid

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:53.845
from a fusion power station
as quickly as possible.

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:55.972
Frankly, the scale of the challenge,

00:00:55.972 --> 00:01:00.351
3,000 gigawatts of fossil fuel to replace.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.020
There's not many things that can do that.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.231
In fact, there may only be
fusion that can really do that.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.484
And while many in the scientific community

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:10.403
predict fusion power will take decades,

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.530
some in the private fusion space

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.241
believe we'll get there
in just a few years,

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.702
as soon as the 2030s.

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:18.912
There is a lot of money

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.039
going into these companies.

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:23.166
And it's very interesting and exciting.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:26.086
There are some I really love

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:28.797
and some I would rather laugh about.

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:30.757
The world is desperately searching

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:33.134
for a replacement for fossil fuels.

00:01:33.134 --> 00:01:35.720
Now scientists and startups

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.306
are betting that a
commercial fusion reactor

00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:39.849
is finally in sight.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:02.205
We are looking at visible light

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:03.373
coming from the plasma.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:08.169
The plasma is this sort of
100,000 degrees temperature,

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.339
but what you actually
see is the coldest part.

00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:13.341
The hottest part you really don't see

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:15.844
because it's too hot to emit light

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.805
in the visible, isn't it?

00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:22.267
Yes.

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:25.061
When I started, I was
doing numerical modeling

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:27.522
and then I realized
that running the machine

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:31.067
was a lot more interesting
and a lot more fun for me.

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:34.696
My work is stressful.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:39.450
My work can be very
demanding in terms of time.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.286
But this is still

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:45.206
one of the most exciting
places in the world.

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:47.208
Here in Oxford, you'll find arguably

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:50.420
the most successful fusion
experiment on Earth,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.381
JET, the Joint European Torus,

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.092
torus being the technical term for donut,

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:57.802
which is how the reactor's shaped.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:01.514
JET's been operating since the early '80s

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:05.602
and only by 1997 were we really
ready to try proper fusion.

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:08.813
And we produced 16 megawatts
of fusion power at the time

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:10.481
which is like a few wind turbines.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:11.566
It's pretty significant.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.401
And it shows that fusion is possible.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.782
The 1997 experiment set records,

00:03:19.782 --> 00:03:23.828
but the reactor was only able
to run for less than a second.

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:26.206
The team spent the next two decades

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:28.166
cooking up a new approach.

00:03:28.166 --> 00:03:31.544
And in 2021, they gave it another shot.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.631
We always knew that we could do better.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.341
The last two days before Christmas

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:38.051
were dedicated to these experiments,

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:41.596
just this window of parameters
where we could get more.

00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:43.306
And we did.

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:45.808
Three, two, one.

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:52.273
JET more than doubled its previous record,

00:03:52.273 --> 00:03:56.527
producing more energy than any
fusion experiment in history.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:57.654
We couldn't hug.

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:00.281
We couldn't high-five, nothing,

00:04:00.281 --> 00:04:03.826
because we have to be at
two meters from each other.

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:07.622
But, you know, it was obvious
that this was a record.

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:08.498
It was successful.

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:10.375
You could see that it was successful.

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:12.835
It's a real step

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:15.672
towards the ultimate promise of fusion,

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.216
a cheap, emissions-free power source

00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:21.344
with virtually unlimited fuel.

00:04:21.344 --> 00:04:24.973
But maybe don't break out
the champagne just yet.

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:27.016
So in the 1997 experiments

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:29.394
we produced a lot of power,
but very transiently.

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:31.604
So it ramped up and then we lost control.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.440
Now we ramp up and
sustain for five seconds.

00:04:35.858 --> 00:04:37.860
So what exactly makes fusion

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.279
such a tough problem

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.698
that sustaining it for five seconds

00:04:41.698 --> 00:04:44.075
constitutes a world record?

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:45.743
Nuclear fusion.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:47.328
Once it's perfected,

00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:50.623
fusion power will give us an
unlimited supply of energy.

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.836
So nuclear fusion is
really what is happening

00:04:54.836 --> 00:04:56.254
inside the sun,

00:04:56.254 --> 00:05:00.300
where lots and lots of hydrogen atoms

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.094
are moving at immense speeds.

00:05:04.262 --> 00:05:07.348
And every so often some
of them fuse together

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:08.599
to form helium.

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:11.894
Now the process at the atomic level

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:15.773
leads to a very small
amount of loss in mass.

00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:18.735
And that little amount of mass

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:21.696
actually generates a lot of energy.

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:23.948
And you do that millions
and millions of times

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:25.033
and you get the sun.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:27.744
Well look, we know fusion works.

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:29.579
It's happening right now in our sun.

00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:30.955
But the reason it's happening in the sun

00:05:30.955 --> 00:05:32.582
is because of the mass of the sun.

00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:33.958
It's so massive,

00:05:33.958 --> 00:05:36.419
has this huge gravitational force

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.796
which is pushing those
isotopes of hydrogen

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:40.506
close enough to fuse.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.843
We obviously cannot
recreate the mass of the sun

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:44.969
here on Earth.

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:49.807
So instead, we have to give
that fuel even more energy.

00:05:49.807 --> 00:05:54.062
So we take a gas, we put a
huge amount of energy into it,

00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:56.647
and that turns it into the
fourth state of matter, plasma.

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:59.275
If you consider water,
for example, it's ice,

00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:00.234
then you warm it up

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:01.527
then you get a fluid,

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:04.030
and then you warm it
further up, you get steam.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.865
And if you then increase
the temperature even further

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:07.325
then you get plasma.

00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:09.869
Common plasmas include lightning,

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:11.162
neon lights,

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:13.456
and these things.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:14.791
We need temperatures

00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:17.585
10 times larger than
in the solar interior.

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:20.338
So this is about 100, 200 million degrees.

00:06:20.338 --> 00:06:21.964
And only at those sort of temperatures

00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:24.050
do you get fusion to happen here on Earth.

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:25.927
Heating something

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:28.137
to 10 times the temperature of the sun

00:06:28.137 --> 00:06:31.766
is, to use the technical term, very hard.

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:35.269
Scientists have been working
at it since the 1930s.

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:36.813
The major breakthrough has been

00:06:36.813 --> 00:06:39.232
the first tokamak experiments in the 1960s

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:41.150
in the former Soviet Union.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.736
They achieved several million degrees,

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.947
and this was a real breakthrough.

00:06:45.947 --> 00:06:48.241
Tokamaks is still one
of the most popular ways

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.659
to create fusion.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:50.952
That's what JET is,

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:53.287
along with many other
government-run reactors

00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:55.081
around the world.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:57.625
Using powerful magnets
to contain the plasma,

00:06:57.625 --> 00:07:00.461
they've achieved temperatures
of 100 million degrees

00:07:00.461 --> 00:07:01.629
and well beyond.

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:05.466
But there's still one big
milestone we need to reach

00:07:05.466 --> 00:07:08.928
before fusion power becomes a reality,

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:09.929
net gain.

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:15.101
We need more power out than
put in to heat the fuel

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.686
in the first place.

00:07:16.686 --> 00:07:18.938
If we can't generate
more power from fusion

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:20.148
than we put in

00:07:20.148 --> 00:07:22.233
then the whole thing's a bust.

00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:24.694
Unfortunately, no one's ever done it,

00:07:24.694 --> 00:07:26.946
not even the brilliant minds at JET.

00:07:27.822 --> 00:07:29.907
That's all kind of part of the plan though

00:07:29.907 --> 00:07:32.702
because JET isn't actually
designed to solve fusion

00:07:32.702 --> 00:07:34.078
all by itself.

00:07:34.078 --> 00:07:37.874
It's just set up for a much
bigger project called ITER.

00:07:38.708 --> 00:07:39.542
Uh, no, no.

00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:40.376
ITER.

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:45.590
We are building this experimental machine

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.468
in the south of France, ITER,
which is going to be big.

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:51.679
And it's the first one that
will produce really more energy

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:53.055
than what it consumes.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.559
ITER is a massive
international collaboration

00:07:56.559 --> 00:07:58.644
between 35 different countries.

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:00.897
And everyone involved
seems pretty confident

00:08:00.897 --> 00:08:04.775
it's going to get to net
gain for the first time.

00:08:04.775 --> 00:08:08.196
So fusion's just around the corner, right?

00:08:08.196 --> 00:08:09.363
Well...

00:08:09.363 --> 00:08:12.033
With the ITER project, the first plasma

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:15.828
is supposed to be created by 2025,

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:20.833
but full fusion reaction
isn't expected until 2035.

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:25.838
So if ITER were the only bet
that we were making on fusion

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.423
then it would be a safe bet to say

00:08:27.423 --> 00:08:30.343
that nuclear fusion is
going to take decades,

00:08:30.343 --> 00:08:34.472
and that's too long for the climate fight.

00:08:34.472 --> 00:08:36.474
Even the most optimistic scientists

00:08:36.474 --> 00:08:39.977
think that fusion power may
not be developed for 50 years,

00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:41.437
if then,

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:42.271
if ever.

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:45.858
Some think we can get there much faster.

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:54.659
There's more than 30
private fusion companies

00:08:54.659 --> 00:08:56.077
around the world.

00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:58.538
And it feels like every month or two

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:01.666
there's another private company
that springs up somewhere

00:09:01.666 --> 00:09:04.710
with another great idea
about how to do this.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.337
The amount of funding going into fusion

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:07.797
has also been scaling.

00:09:07.797 --> 00:09:11.008
There's more private funding
going into fusion per year

00:09:11.008 --> 00:09:12.510
than there is federal government funding

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:13.886
in the United States.

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:16.472
The private fusion space is still small

00:09:16.472 --> 00:09:18.558
in terms of budget and people

00:09:18.558 --> 00:09:20.351
when compared to the mainstream.

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:23.020
But if you look at the rate of progress,

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.356
I would say it's much, much faster.

00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:29.360
And I do think that it
will be the private side

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.571
that produces the first vital technology.

00:09:37.493 --> 00:09:39.954
Canadian fusion company General Fusion

00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:43.708
is steering away from the
traditional tokamak design.

00:09:43.708 --> 00:09:45.960
If you have followed fusion
at all in recent years

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.297
you might have come across
this steampunk octopus,

00:09:49.297 --> 00:09:52.174
an earlier prototype of their reactor.

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:54.135
It's very analogous to

00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:57.471
a fusion version of a diesel engine.

00:09:57.471 --> 00:09:58.806
So you basically,

00:09:58.806 --> 00:10:00.766
you have this very large cavity

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:03.394
that's opened up inside of liquid metal.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.314
And into that liquid metal

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:10.526
we inject a high temperature
plasma of hydrogen.

00:10:11.527 --> 00:10:15.698
We can now perform this
compression to heat up that plasma

00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:19.201
very much the same way
you think about a piston

00:10:19.201 --> 00:10:22.455
compressing and heating the
fuel in a diesel engine.

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:25.124
This is a steam-driven compression process

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:27.543
using an array of drivers.

00:10:27.543 --> 00:10:31.255
It compresses and heats
this magnetized plasma

00:10:31.255 --> 00:10:33.424
to fusion conditions.

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:35.468
General Fusion's reactor

00:10:35.468 --> 00:10:38.095
would create brief
bursts of fusion energy,

00:10:38.095 --> 00:10:41.057
an approach they hope will
achieve net gain more easily

00:10:41.057 --> 00:10:42.808
than a tokamak.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:47.188
Founded in 2002, and supported
by investors like Jeff Bezos,

00:10:47.188 --> 00:10:48.689
they're one of the furthest along

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:51.025
in the field of fusion startups

00:10:51.025 --> 00:10:55.112
with plans to build a demonstrative
plant by 2026 in Oxford

00:10:55.112 --> 00:10:58.157
on the same scientific campus as JET.

00:10:58.157 --> 00:11:00.660
This thing won't actually
put megawatts on the grid,

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.996
but it will prove that
our approach to fusion

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:06.207
in a power plant relevant environment

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:07.958
can actually make fusion happen.

00:11:08.876 --> 00:11:11.128
Aiming to break ground
in a matter of months,

00:11:11.128 --> 00:11:13.172
the company is running full tilt

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:15.341
to work out all the kinks before showtime.

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:18.844
This is about 2/3 of the full scale

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:21.472
that we'll need in our
fusion demonstration plant.

00:11:21.472 --> 00:11:23.766
So it's mostly at this point

00:11:23.766 --> 00:11:27.186
a question of understanding
the properties of the plasma

00:11:27.186 --> 00:11:28.187
and the plasma physics,

00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:30.398
to be confident that that will scale

00:11:30.398 --> 00:11:32.108
as we build the larger version.

00:11:33.818 --> 00:11:35.820
The alarms you hear when we start charging

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:36.946
are alerting you to the fact

00:11:36.946 --> 00:11:39.990
that we're starting to
charge this machine.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.660
You hear a ping from when the plasma

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.829
is interacting with the
wall of the machine.

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:55.005
You see, at the time you heard that thump,

00:11:55.005 --> 00:11:57.425
that's when the machine
created the plasma.

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:01.011
It's really exciting to see

00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:05.057
what's happening in the
private fusion industry.

00:12:05.057 --> 00:12:07.017
Of course, I came to General Fusion

00:12:07.017 --> 00:12:09.729
because I like General
Fusion's technology.

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:11.814
That's one man's opinion.

00:12:11.814 --> 00:12:13.441
I think we have,

00:12:13.441 --> 00:12:16.569
my view is the first
really good shot on goal.

00:12:16.569 --> 00:12:18.279
But what really feels good

00:12:18.279 --> 00:12:20.573
is there's going to be lots
of shots on goal, right?

00:12:20.573 --> 00:12:22.408
And I'm very confident

00:12:22.408 --> 00:12:24.410
we're going to score the win that we need.

00:12:26.954 --> 00:12:28.956
Not far from General
Fusion's headquarters,

00:12:28.956 --> 00:12:32.042
you can find another fusion company.

00:12:32.042 --> 00:12:34.170
Helion Energy, one of
the most buzzed about

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.796
fusion companies in the world

00:12:35.796 --> 00:12:37.923
is hunkered down in some
former Boeing hangers

00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:39.133
just outside Seattle.

00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:41.927
What we have tried to do at Helion

00:12:41.927 --> 00:12:44.138
is approach fusion from
a different direction

00:12:44.138 --> 00:12:45.723
than a lot of other people.

00:12:45.723 --> 00:12:47.433
We looked at the state of the art

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:49.351
of what was being built in fusion

00:12:49.351 --> 00:12:51.729
and thought really there
has to be a better way

00:12:51.729 --> 00:12:53.606
to get to commercial fusion faster.

00:12:54.815 --> 00:12:56.400
That might sound like hype,

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.903
but there really is
something radically different

00:12:58.903 --> 00:13:00.279
about Helion's reactor.

00:13:01.238 --> 00:13:04.742
Almost every idea behind
a nuclear fusion startup

00:13:04.742 --> 00:13:05.576
or a company

00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:09.455
is to rely on the heat that
is generated by fusion.

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:13.667
And then converting that heat into steam,

00:13:13.667 --> 00:13:16.796
which is then used to turn
turbines to generate electricity.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:20.966
Now, Helion says that it
doesn't have to go through

00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:24.178
that heating and turning a turbine phase.

00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:27.556
So we do something called
direct energy conversion

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:30.392
where we take the magnetic
energy of the fusion system,

00:13:30.392 --> 00:13:32.561
the charged particle energy of the fuel,

00:13:32.561 --> 00:13:35.231
and directly extract that to electricity.

00:13:35.231 --> 00:13:36.482
We inject our fuel.

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:39.568
We magnetically compress that fusion fuel.

00:13:39.568 --> 00:13:40.694
Fusion begins.

00:13:40.694 --> 00:13:43.155
It pushes back on that magnetic field.

00:13:43.155 --> 00:13:46.325
So, a good analogy is
regenerative braking in your car.

00:13:46.325 --> 00:13:48.369
We then directly regeneratively

00:13:48.369 --> 00:13:51.080
take the electricity back
out of that fusion expansion

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.623
and turn that into electricity.

00:13:53.791 --> 00:13:56.377
By cutting out the steam step for fusion

00:13:56.377 --> 00:13:59.672
we can radically increase the
overall engineering efficiency

00:13:59.672 --> 00:14:00.840
of the system.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.717
We aim for a system now
that can be much smaller,

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:06.387
required a lot less of the
complexity and the challenges,

00:14:06.387 --> 00:14:09.723
and really, from my point of
view, a lot less of the time.

00:14:10.599 --> 00:14:11.851
That pitch was good enough

00:14:11.851 --> 00:14:15.062
to get Silicon Valley giants
Sam Altman and Peter Thiel

00:14:15.062 --> 00:14:17.064
on board as investors.

00:14:17.064 --> 00:14:20.401
The company's $500 million Series E round

00:14:20.401 --> 00:14:22.945
makes them one of the
best-funded fusion companies

00:14:22.945 --> 00:14:24.822
in the world.

00:14:24.822 --> 00:14:27.867
They're also making one of
the most ambitious predictions

00:14:27.867 --> 00:14:29.201
in the industry.

00:14:29.201 --> 00:14:32.580
The goal is to get this built by 2024,

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.832
running, generating net
electricity from fusion

00:14:34.832 --> 00:14:36.542
for the first time.

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:37.710
Timeline is the driver.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.128
It's always the driver.

00:14:39.128 --> 00:14:40.379
And so if it's a question of,

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:41.672
well, it could be a little bit better,

00:14:41.672 --> 00:14:43.048
but take an extra year,

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:45.009
we say, no, we're going to
make it a little bit worse

00:14:45.009 --> 00:14:47.052
but get it done a year earlier.

00:14:47.052 --> 00:14:48.554
It's the Silicon Valley mentality

00:14:48.554 --> 00:14:51.557
of how can you build as
absolutely as fast as possible?

00:14:57.146 --> 00:14:58.439
Yeah, help me with the gas pressure.

00:14:58.439 --> 00:14:59.523
Everything's isolated.

00:15:00.774 --> 00:15:01.942
You got the gas pressure.

00:15:03.027 --> 00:15:04.028
Fire.
Firing.

00:15:06.989 --> 00:15:07.823
Nice.

00:15:07.823 --> 00:15:09.366
Good shot.

00:15:09.366 --> 00:15:10.200
That was easy.

00:15:11.368 --> 00:15:12.453
Back in Oxford,

00:15:12.453 --> 00:15:14.955
First Light Fusion is taking what might be

00:15:14.955 --> 00:15:18.208
the most original approach
of any fusion company,

00:15:18.208 --> 00:15:19.793
borrowing from a branch of fusion

00:15:19.793 --> 00:15:21.712
called inertial confinement.

00:15:23.672 --> 00:15:28.177
The idea behind inertial
confinement is you hold plasma

00:15:28.177 --> 00:15:30.638
for a very short time

00:15:30.638 --> 00:15:32.556
in a very small space.

00:15:32.556 --> 00:15:35.225
So, one example of inertial confinement

00:15:35.225 --> 00:15:38.145
is where lasers, very high-powered lasers,

00:15:38.145 --> 00:15:41.357
are used to heat a very
small amount of hydrogen

00:15:41.357 --> 00:15:45.402
for nanoseconds, that is
billionths of a second.

00:15:45.402 --> 00:15:46.946
This has been done before,

00:15:46.946 --> 00:15:49.657
most famously at the
National Ignition Facility

00:15:49.657 --> 00:15:51.158
in California.

00:15:51.158 --> 00:15:54.370
But First Light has come
up with a new approach.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:55.829
We call it projectile fusion.

00:15:55.829 --> 00:15:57.957
We have a high velocity projectile.

00:15:57.957 --> 00:16:01.502
It flies in and it hits into
our, what we call a target.

00:16:01.502 --> 00:16:04.713
And the target has to focus
the energy of the projectile

00:16:04.713 --> 00:16:06.131
into the fusion fuel.

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:07.591
This is one of our targets.

00:16:08.926 --> 00:16:11.553
This is the key technology
to our approach to fusion.

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:15.099
So this is completely turned into a plasma

00:16:15.099 --> 00:16:17.935
by the force of the impact
and the energy released.

00:16:20.646 --> 00:16:23.232
In a power plant, one of these targets

00:16:23.232 --> 00:16:25.859
would release enough energy
to power the average UK home

00:16:25.859 --> 00:16:28.112
for over two years.

00:16:28.112 --> 00:16:29.154
That sound.

00:16:30.739 --> 00:16:33.659
That's the projectile being
fired out of this gas gun

00:16:33.659 --> 00:16:36.745
at around 15,000 miles an hour.

00:16:36.745 --> 00:16:37.705
To generate power

00:16:37.705 --> 00:16:39.665
you have to do that at a repetition rate.

00:16:39.665 --> 00:16:41.917
You have to do a certain number

00:16:41.917 --> 00:16:44.461
and it's the energy you release every time

00:16:44.461 --> 00:16:45.421
times by the frequency

00:16:45.421 --> 00:16:46.588
and that's the power.

00:16:46.588 --> 00:16:48.090
So in our power plant design

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:50.634
we'd be doing this about
once every 30 seconds.

00:16:51.468 --> 00:16:54.763
So we recently showed fusion in our lab

00:16:54.763 --> 00:16:57.307
with a projectile driven
approach for the first time.

00:16:57.307 --> 00:16:59.518
Just simply experimentally
it's a big proof of concept

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:00.894
that it can really work.

00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:04.314
If we look at the actual
amount of fusion we produced

00:17:04.314 --> 00:17:06.900
then that number by
itself, it was 50 neutrons.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.193
And we're not hiding it.

00:17:08.193 --> 00:17:09.570
It's not very impressive.

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:10.738
But the point is

00:17:10.738 --> 00:17:12.990
that's exactly what the
simulations predicted.

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:16.702
And that's what gives us
the very rapid, we hope,

00:17:16.702 --> 00:17:20.372
pathway forwards to improve that number.

00:17:20.372 --> 00:17:22.916
I hope we're not talking about 50 neutrons

00:17:22.916 --> 00:17:24.043
at the end of the year.

00:17:27.963 --> 00:17:29.548
So there's these disruptive technologies

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:31.717
which are coming into our
space, which is great.

00:17:31.717 --> 00:17:32.760
We want new ideas.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.470
We want people to come and tackle

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:36.013
the big challenges that we face.

00:17:36.013 --> 00:17:37.806
There's lots of startup companies.

00:17:37.806 --> 00:17:39.183
Some of them will fail.

00:17:39.183 --> 00:17:40.476
Some of them will go to the wall.

00:17:40.476 --> 00:17:42.394
Some of them will wildly succeed.

00:17:42.394 --> 00:17:44.354
That's just healthy.

00:17:44.354 --> 00:17:46.899
Some on the scientific
research side of fusion

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:49.943
are more critical of
the startup phenomenon.

00:17:49.943 --> 00:17:52.529
Few private fusion companies
have shown any results

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:54.823
beyond fusing a few atoms.

00:17:54.823 --> 00:17:57.493
And many scientists
worry that these startups

00:17:57.493 --> 00:17:59.078
are promising too much.

00:17:59.912 --> 00:18:01.705
The cons in my mind

00:18:01.705 --> 00:18:04.416
are the promises by companies

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:05.959
who either have a concept

00:18:05.959 --> 00:18:08.253
that we have already, for
good reason, put apart

00:18:08.253 --> 00:18:10.798
and knew why they won't work,

00:18:10.798 --> 00:18:13.175
or completely new concepts.

00:18:13.175 --> 00:18:16.386
Some change them every other year.

00:18:16.386 --> 00:18:18.722
And then they still promise fusion energy

00:18:18.722 --> 00:18:21.266
by 2030 or something.

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:23.435
And I don't like that too
much because I'm afraid

00:18:23.435 --> 00:18:27.272
that if there are so
many promises not kept

00:18:27.272 --> 00:18:29.942
that this would not be
positive for fusion energy

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:31.568
in general.

00:18:31.568 --> 00:18:35.948
If you look at the timescales
that governments work at

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.617
in research,

00:18:38.617 --> 00:18:41.036
that's completely different
than the timescales

00:18:41.036 --> 00:18:43.956
that entrepreneurial
private industry works at.

00:18:43.956 --> 00:18:46.375
It's not that there's a
problem there with government,

00:18:46.375 --> 00:18:49.211
but, you know, they're a
different set of metrics there.

00:18:49.211 --> 00:18:52.840
They're largely
research-driven organizations.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:56.677
You need look no further than
commercial access to space.

00:18:56.677 --> 00:18:57.803
SpaceX.

00:18:57.803 --> 00:19:01.306
They've created what people
thought would never be possible,

00:19:01.306 --> 00:19:03.809
which is reusable rockets.

00:19:03.809 --> 00:19:06.145
NASA was never able to achieve that

00:19:06.145 --> 00:19:09.982
because they weren't
motivated by the same things.

00:19:09.982 --> 00:19:13.861
And so when it comes to the
last mile of commercialization,

00:19:13.861 --> 00:19:16.113
that's where you really
need to hand the baton

00:19:16.113 --> 00:19:18.490
from government to private industry.

00:19:19.658 --> 00:19:21.994
From what I know, some
of the private sector

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:23.579
is very serious

00:19:23.579 --> 00:19:25.164
and is bringing

00:19:25.164 --> 00:19:30.002
significant capital and
significant engineering advances.

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:33.213
It needs to promise things

00:19:33.213 --> 00:19:34.047
on the time

00:19:34.047 --> 00:19:35.340
on a realistic timescale.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:36.758
So, not next year.

00:19:37.593 --> 00:19:40.721
And the timescale of about
20 years is realistic.

00:19:42.723 --> 00:19:44.016
It remains to be seen

00:19:44.016 --> 00:19:46.685
whether the Silicon
Valley VC type approach

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:49.605
will actually get us to
commercial fusion faster.

00:19:51.607 --> 00:19:53.525
One thing is for sure though,

00:19:53.525 --> 00:19:57.738
progress, however slow and
incremental, is being made.

00:19:57.738 --> 00:20:00.866
And if we're going to get
to a fusion powered future,

00:20:00.866 --> 00:20:04.328
we're probably going to need
both the painstaking research

00:20:04.328 --> 00:20:06.163
and the risky new ideas.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.707
I think the rate of
progress is really driven

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:09.791
by the imperative.

00:20:09.791 --> 00:20:12.961
And to be honest, I mean, let's
just be honest as a society,

00:20:12.961 --> 00:20:14.254
over the last 20 or 30 years

00:20:14.254 --> 00:20:16.340
we've been pretty comfortable
with burning fossil fuels.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:18.508
The fact that we have to stop it

00:20:18.508 --> 00:20:20.260
now drives the imperative.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.471
This is the most important
question for mankind

00:20:22.471 --> 00:20:25.057
because, I mean, you need
electricity or energy

00:20:25.057 --> 00:20:26.558
for everything.

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:28.477
We have a lot of developing countries,

00:20:28.477 --> 00:20:29.645
and if these countries

00:20:29.645 --> 00:20:31.355
want to come to our standards of living,

00:20:31.355 --> 00:20:34.566
there's no way other than
provide them with cheap energy,

00:20:34.566 --> 00:20:36.318
and better, CO2 neutral.

00:20:36.318 --> 00:20:38.320
Otherwise, we have a climate crisis.

00:20:39.571 --> 00:20:41.198
Each fusion technology

00:20:41.198 --> 00:20:43.158
has a slightly different flavor

00:20:43.158 --> 00:20:46.119
in terms of its ultimate
value proposition.

00:20:46.119 --> 00:20:48.997
And so I think that you're going to see

00:20:48.997 --> 00:20:52.292
a portfolio of technologies
being commercialized

00:20:52.292 --> 00:20:53.835
over the coming decades

00:20:53.835 --> 00:20:55.963
that address different
parts of the market.

00:20:55.963 --> 00:20:58.548
And the market is huge and diverse.

00:20:58.548 --> 00:21:01.927
The total addressable market for fusion

00:21:01.927 --> 00:21:04.888
is on the order of $1 trillion a year.

00:21:04.888 --> 00:21:05.931
It might be decades

00:21:05.931 --> 00:21:08.600
before fusion becomes a reality,

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:13.021
but it's the kind of
cheap, unlimited power

00:21:13.021 --> 00:21:16.566
that you would want to give a civilization

00:21:16.566 --> 00:21:19.027
to be able to do all
the things it wants to.

00:21:20.946 --> 00:21:23.532
Imagine if you could
make a lot of rocket fuel

00:21:23.532 --> 00:21:25.534
to create an entire fleet of rockets

00:21:25.534 --> 00:21:27.369
that would go and mine asteroids

00:21:27.369 --> 00:21:28.745
because, you know, at some point

00:21:28.745 --> 00:21:30.247
we're going to run out of metals.

00:21:31.206 --> 00:21:32.374
Or you could remove

00:21:32.374 --> 00:21:35.085
existing carbon dioxide in the atmosphere

00:21:35.085 --> 00:21:37.838
back down and bury it deep underground.

00:21:37.838 --> 00:21:41.091
That process requires a ton of energy,

00:21:41.091 --> 00:21:43.802
but if you were able to
create nuclear fusion

00:21:43.802 --> 00:21:46.680
you could imagine restoring
the Earth's atmosphere

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:48.765
to pre-industrial levels.

00:21:49.808 --> 00:21:51.351
And these are the kinds of application

00:21:51.351 --> 00:21:54.146
that really cheap and clean energy

00:21:54.146 --> 00:21:56.440
can enable humanity to do.

00:22:00.569 --> 00:22:03.947
I want my kids to have a future

00:22:03.947 --> 00:22:07.492
that has the possibilities that I've had.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.705
So I hope we get fusion,

00:22:11.705 --> 00:22:14.082
and I think private, public,

00:22:14.082 --> 00:22:16.835
it all needs to come together
because this is worth it.

